data: {"delta":{"partial_json":"{\"file_path\": \"/home/<USER:1>/.local/share/cs-campaign/output/mission-report.md\", \"content\": \"# Mission report: greet, tested\\n\\n## Outcome\\n\\nMission satisfied. dev delivered and I independently verified all three\\ncriteria on dev's branch `cs-sandbox/<DEV:1>.<GROUP:1>`\\n(commit `f05ce88`):\\n\\n1. `src/greet.py` — `greet(name)` returns `f\\\"Hello, {name}!\\\"`.\\n2. `tests/test_greet.py` — covers a normal name (`\\\"World\\\"`) and the empty\\n   string (`\\\"\\\"`).\\n3. `python3 -m unittest discover -s tests` exits 0.\\n\\n## Verification method\\n\\nI did not rely on dev's self-report. I fetched dev's branch\\n(`cs-campaign-member fetch dev`), added a git worktree at that commit\\n(`f05ce88`), and ran `python3 -m unittest discover -s tests` myself from\\nthat worktree:\\n\\n```\\n..\\n----------------------------------------------------------------------\\nRan 2 tests in 0.000s\\n\\nOK\\nEXIT CODE: 0\\n```\\n\\nThis matched dev's reported output exactly. Worktree removed after\\nverification.\\n\\n## Timeline\\n\\n- d001 (briefing confirmation, both orchestrator and dev): accepted.\\n- d002 (implementation dispatch to dev): accepted after independent\\n  verification. No rework needed — first attempt was correct and complete.\\n\\n## Notes\\n\\nAs orchestrator I wrote no code myself (per role brief); the deliverable\\nlives entirely on dev's branch. My own branch\\n(`cs-sandbox/<ORCHESTRATOR:1>.<GROUP:1>`) is expected to be\\nunchanged from base — orchestration work is recorded here and in the\\ncampaign's own reply/dispatch history, not as source commits.\\n\"}","type":"input_json_delta"},"index":1,"type":"content_block_delta"}

